<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    INERT. Sublime loads .tmPreferences, not .tmPreferences.disabled, so this
    file does nothing where it sits. Note that .tmPreferences ARE found
    recursively inside a package, so moving it to a subdirectory would NOT
    disable it; the extension is the switch.

    WHAT IT IS FOR

    'Reference List - Wiki Body.tmPreferences' subtracts markup.heading and
    entity.name.tag.todo, which costs roughly 700 real backlinks: a wiki link
    written inside a heading or inside an open todo is not indexed, so
    'Wiki: Show backlinks' does not list it and 'Wiki: Rename note and
    backlinks' does not rewrite it.

    The subtractions are there because at any point only the highest scoring
    .tmPreferences entry applies, and the wiki entry outscores both container
    entries. Since the wiki entry keeps its points out of both symbol lists,
    winning inside a heading or a todo cut that container's symbol in half.
    The reported symptom was a project symbol called ']]'.

    This file is the way out, IF an undocumented question happens to fall the
    right way: does Sublime build a symbol region out of adjacent points that
    are merely all FLAGGED for a list, or only out of adjacent points owned by
    the same ENTRY? Nothing in the API reference or in symbols documentation
    says, and no headless test reproduces the indexer, so the only way to know
    is to look.

    HOW TO SETTLE IT (about a minute)

      1. Rename this file to drop the '.disabled' suffix.
      2. Open a note with a todo that contains a wiki link (todoUNDP.md has
         about 300) and press ctrl+R.
           * one entry per todo, whole   -> FLAGGED wins. Good outcome.
           * fragments such as '[[', a bare name, ']] and then'  -> ENTRY wins.
      3. Open meetingsFY2324.md and press ctrl+R, then ctrl+shift+R, and look
         at a '## people: [[a]] [[b]]' heading for the same thing.
      4. If FLAGGED won: delete '- markup.heading - entity.name.tag.todo' from
         the selector in 'Reference List - Wiki Body.tmPreferences', keep this
         file enabled, drop HEADING_NOTE from WikiLinks.py, and re-measure the
         reference counts in CLAUDE.md. Consider splitting this entry in two so
         the todo half can keep showInIndexedSymbolList 0.
         If ENTRY won: put the '.disabled' back. Nothing is worse than it was;
         the containers were already fragmented when the bug was reported.

    WHAT IT DOES DIFFERENTLY

    It matches wiki link targets only where they sit INSIDE one of the two
    containers, which makes it outscore both the container entry and the plain
    body entry, and it sets every list flag to 1 instead of 0. So the points
    stay flagged for the symbol lists their container wanted them in, and are
    additionally offered as references.

    The known cost, even in the good outcome: bare target names reach the
    DEFINITION namespace, which is flat and shared with the '_note' tokens and
    with MarkdownEditing's '#'-prefixed headings. A note called 'cristina'
    would then answer both a definition and a reference lookup for 'cristina'.
    Splitting this entry in two (headings keep showInIndexedSymbolList 1, todos
    go back to 0) confines that to headings, which is the point of step 4.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>name</key><string>Wiki Link References (inside headings and todos)</string>

    <key>scope</key>
    <string>text.html.markdown markup.heading meta.link.reference.wiki.description.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.begin.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.end.markdown, text.html.markdown entity.name.tag.todo meta.link.reference.wiki.description.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.begin.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.end.markdown</string>

    <key>settings</key>
    <dict>
        <key>showInSymbolList</key><integer>1</integer>
        <key>showInIndexedSymbolList</key><integer>1</integer>
        <key>showInIndexedReferenceList</key><integer>1</integer>
        <key>kind</key><string>markup.list</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
